  "description": "Method replication: the pre-test of a photograph survey on urban meadows and lawns in Tubingen, Germany, with the paper's seven photographs (five natural meadows and two lawns, taken standing on cloudy days and not digitally edited) of green spaces managed under the colourful meadow initiative Bunte Wiese Tubingen. For each photograph, shown one at a time, participants answer the paper's four questions: (I) What are your first impressions on looking at this photograph? (open answer); (II) How do you assess this picture: attractive, spruce (e.g., horticultural, proper and tidy maintenance), enriching (e.g., educational, functional, social benefits) for urban areas, each on a five-level Likert item; (III) Would you change anything and, if so, what?; and (IV) Would you like to have more meadows like this? Why - why not? They then answer the paper's six questions on personal attitude (role of nature in their life, access to a garden, using nature for relaxation, observing plants and animals, knowledge of insects, active nature protection) and its demographic topics (sex, age, education, discipline). Rebuilt from Unterweger, Schrode & Betz (2017), Urban Nature: Perception and Acceptance of Alternative Green Space Management and the Change of Awareness after Provision of Environmental Information. A Chance for Biodiversity Protection, Urban Science 1(3): 24, with the photographs from its Figure 1A and the questions from Section 2.1 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: P1-P7 in the order of Figure 1A (P2 and P5 are lawns); answers keyed by image id are in impression, assess (item codes attractive, spruce, enriching), change, change_what, more and more_why; each image's type is stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: means of the three Likert items for meadows versus lawns per participant compared with paired t-tests, one-way ANOVA with Bonferroni tests of the ratings across behavioural and demographic groups, shares of yes and no for the change and more-meadows questions, and open answers coded into topics in the manner of grounded theory. The paper (424 complete responses, recruited through the University of Tubingen e-mail lists and the initiative's Facebook page in 2015) found meadows rated more attractive and enriching but less spruce than lawns, most participants wanting no change and more such meadows, and ratings of the meadows' value for wildlife lower among people who never observe plants and animals. Differences from the original study: (1) only the pre-test is replicated; the paper's information panels from the initiative and its post-test with eight pictures (including an unnatural seed-mix meadow) are left out, because the panel texts are not printed; (2) the paper does not print the scale labels; the five-level labels for question II (1 = not at all, 5 = very much), the six-level labels for the role of nature, the frequency labels for the garden, relaxation and nature-protection questions and the demographic answer options are ours; the frequency labels for observing plants and animals (daily, often, sometimes, rarely, never) and the insect knowledge options (no, limited, yes) follow the paper; (3) the order of the photographs is randomised here; the paper does not say whether it was; (4) the instruction text is ours and the typo asses in question II is corrected; (5) questions III and IV are split here into a yes/no answer and an optional open answer, as the paper reports both the yes and no votes and the comments; (6) the paper ran on Unipark and does not state the language of its questions; here the survey is in English and anyone can take part; (7) the photographs are the figure copies (about 456-576 px wide).",
